--TEST--
Core\Crypto::deriveKey and ::expandKey answer a `secret bytes`, so neither can be put in a plain `bytes` — the qualifier is the answer's type and not a property of where the key came from — `rule:security/secret-qualifier`, `rule:core-api/tier-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The half of `crypto-every-member-that-answers-a-key-answers-one-both-ciphers-take`
// that no running case can assert: that these two answer the *qualified* type.
// A derivation answering a plain `bytes` would round-trip, seal and open, and
// pass every case in this suite — and would let a key reach `echo`, a log line
// and a `Throwable` message, which is the whole of what the qualifier buys.
// `Core\Crypto::generateKey` is pinned the same way by the sinks under
// tests/conformance/reject/; assignment widens onto the bit and narrows through
// it never, so this is the direction that reports.
bytes $salt = Core\Bytes::fill(16, 65);
bytes $stretched = Core\Crypto::deriveKey("correct horse battery staple", $salt, 100000);
bytes $expanded = Core\Crypto::expandKey(Core\Crypto::generateKey(), $salt, "encryption");
echo Core\Bytes::length($stretched), Core\Bytes::length($expanded), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `secret bytes`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
